00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.800
For Kids in theater can be
a life-changing experience

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.230
opening up new worlds and emotions.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.280
The same is also true

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.440
for those living on the autism spectrum.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.540
That's why a Philadelphia
theater has created

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.390
a unique drama program that
helps people on the spectrum

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.360
foster empathy and create
tools for expression.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.960
We have the Executive Artistic
Director of Theater Horizon

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.920
on the line right now,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.990
along with the Director
of Community Investment,

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.970
they watch on NBC10 Philadelphia.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.857
Everybody say hello to
Nell and Mydera, hi.

00:00:42.748 --> 00:00:44.210
(audience cheering)

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.043
- Hey.

00:00:45.043 --> 00:00:45.876
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:45.876 --> 00:00:47.070
- Nice to see y'all.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.960
So how did this all get started, Nell?

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.090
- Sure. Yeah, so Theater Horizon,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.280
we're always trying to find
new ways to help people

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.050
feel like the theater is for them.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.040
And 18 years ago,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.260
we had a really unique
opportunity to do that.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.520
Some parents came to us

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.250
after their son just
had an autism diagnosis,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.040
which people talked about
a lot less 18 years ago.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.930
And they wanted to
build his social skills,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.190
his emotional intelligence

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.440
and so we built a program
from the ground up.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.050
It started with one kid
and now 18 years later,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.710
we have 60 kids who work
with us throughout the year.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.320
It's been a transformative
experience for their families

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.210
and I'm really proud of
the fact that actually

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.970
six of our autism drama
students just performed

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.040
in our last professional production.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.840
- Oh, my gosh.
(audience cheering)

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.560
Can I just say, you
didn't have to say yes,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.393
you know what I'm saying?

00:01:32.393 --> 00:01:34.050
You could have said, "Oh,
we don't provide that."

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:34.883
You could have said,

00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:37.110
"Oh, I'm so sorry we
don't have that here."

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.350
But you didn't, you reworked
everything to accommodate

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.860
and because of that, your whole life grows

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.510
and changes in the way you didn't expect.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.710
And that's a beautiful thing.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.990
I just want to commend you
both on that, that's amazing.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.228
So Mydera, explain how it works.

00:01:52.228 --> 00:01:54.240
(audience cheering)

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.073
- Sure thing.

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:57.240
Typically our program
runs three times a year,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.550
in the fall, spring, and in the summer.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.560
Our students can join us in-person

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.410
or they can tune in on
our virtual classes,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.530
really whatever way they feel
most comfortable to engage.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.050
And then through theater
games, creative play,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.750
interaction and improv, we
create a social environment

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.790
where our students are
encouraged to further

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.900
their social relationships,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.390
grow their social skills
and flexible thinking

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.330
and find new and creative
ways to express themselves.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.520
And these are the type of
skills that are fundamental

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.440
when you're talking
about social engagement

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.970
and social interaction.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.530
And we know there are theater games

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.080
and our opportunities to have
this time with our student,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.880
they are monumental in making sure

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.800
that moments and realizations like this

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.430
for that particular student and
so many others are possible.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.050
- Man, teaching a kid the
skill of how to communicate

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.870
how they feel on the
inside is so invaluable.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.420
So many kids do have
a hard time with that.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.820
That's such an incredible
gift that you're giving

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.190
and such an incredible opportunity too.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.050
And helping these
parents, this is so cool.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.100
Thank you so much, Nell.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.420
Thank you Mydera.
(audience cheering)

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.956
I'm liking your posts right
now, I'm loving y'all even more.

00:03:00.956 --> 00:03:01.789
